==============================================================================
WARNINGS ON THE INVENTORY ITSELF
==============================================================================

  1. THE INVENTORY DESCRIBES THE linux/amd64 WHEELS
     Where a distribution publishes platform wheels, the license facts in
     LICENSE were taken from the manylinux x86_64 wheel, because that is what
     pip installs in this image. An image built for linux/arm64 would resolve
     to different wheels, and its vendored-library set has NOT been checked
     here.

  2. certifi SHIPS A POINTER TO THE MPL, NOT A COPY OF IT
     certifi's license file is a short notice saying the Source Code Form is
     subject to the MPL 2.0 and giving a URL. The MPL requires that a copy of
     the license accompany the distribution. Since this LICENSE records
     licenses by reference rather than reproducing their full texts, a full
     MPL-2.0 copy is NOT present in the source tree. Decide with the PPMC
     whether the reference is sufficient for a convenience binary, or add the
     full MPL-2.0 text to the release.

  3. THREE DISTRIBUTIONS ARE INSTALLED WITHOUT ANY VERSION CONSTRAINT
     The build runs

         pip3 install --upgrade pip six wheel virtualenv

     before installing the pinned requirements. six, wheel and virtualenv --
     and virtualenv's own dependencies -- are therefore whatever the index
     serves on the day of the build, and are NOT part of the inventory in
     LICENSE except for six, which requirements.txt also pins. The two steps
     contradict each other, exactly as they do in the sibling Python runtimes.
     Pin them, or drop them and let requirements.txt decide.

  4. THE PINNED REQUIREMENTS ARE DETERMINISTIC, THE INVENTORY IS NOT
     SELF-MAINTAINING
     requirements.txt is generated by pip-compile from requirements.in and
     pins all 81 distributions with ==, so the inventory is exact for as long
     as that file is not regenerated. Regenerating it can introduce new
     transitive distributions whose licenses nobody has reviewed. Add a license
     check to whatever regenerates requirements.txt, and regenerate LICENSE,
     NOTICE and this file in the same step.

  5. ONE DISTRIBUTION SHIPS NO LICENSE TEXT
     langsmith 0.12.4 declares MIT in its metadata but publishes no license
     file in either its wheel or its sdist, so there is no copyright notice to
     reproduce, which is what the MIT license requires of a redistributor.

  6. VENDORED BINARY CODE IS ONLY AS VISIBLE AS THE WHEEL MAKES IT
     A wheel is under no obligation to declare the shared objects it
     vendors. pydantic-core, jiter, orjson, ormsgpack, zstandard, xxhash,
     uuid-utils and others ship
     compiled code built from vendored C, C++ and Rust projects, and no claim
     is made here that every vendored component is accounted for.

  7. WHEEL METADATA WAS READ FROM PyPI, NOT FROM A BUILT IMAGE
     The license facts recorded in LICENSE were taken from the wheels and
     sdists published on PyPI at exactly the versions pinned in
     requirements.txt. The authoritative copy for the shipped image remains
     the <dist>-<version>.dist-info/ directory inside the image.

  8. THE BUNDLED PROXY CANNOT BE VERIFIED FROM THIS DIRECTORY
     /bin/proxy is copied from the image named by the COMMON build argument,
     which defaults to `missing:missing`. The Go module inventory was derived
     from the go.mod and go.sum of this repository, not from the binary that
     actually ends up in the image.

  9. LICENSE RECORDS LICENSES BY REFERENCE, NOT BY REPRODUCTION
     Following the layout of the Apache Pekko LICENSE and NOTICE

         https://github.com/apache/pekko/blob/main/LICENSE
         https://github.com/apache/pekko/blob/main/NOTICE

     each component block names the license and reproduces the copyright
     notice, rather than reproducing the full license text. This satisfies the
     attribution requirement of the MIT and BSD families, whose condition is
     that the copyright notice be retained. Confirm with the mentors that it is
     also acceptable for the MPL components in Category B above.

 10. DEPENDENCIES OF USER ACTIONS ARE NOT COVERED
     Beyond the distributions listed above, user actions bring their own
     Python dependencies at deploy time. Those libraries are NOT part of this
     image and NOT part of any Apache release artifact, and no claim is made
     here about their licenses.


==============================================================================
ADDITIONAL NOTES
==============================================================================

On top of the default package set shared by all the Python runtimes, this
image adds the data and LLM stack declared in requirements.in: beautifulsoup4,
feedparser, joblib, plotly, openai, ollama,
langchain, nltk and langdetect, together with their transitive dependencies.

lxml arrives here as the Debian python3-lxml package, not as a wheel.

The pinned set is identical to that of the sibling versioned Python
runtimes: with lightgbm removed, all three resolve to the same 78
distributions at the same versions.
